A slab allocator
================

One region of bytes carved into fixed blocks, with the free list threaded through the free blocks'
own storage.

The axis this owns is **raw storage** — reinterpreting bytes as a typed pointer, asking what a type's
storage costs, and the address arithmetic between the two. Nothing else in the set touches any of it.
`guide/kernel` is allocator-free but never *makes* storage; it is handed three fixed tables and
indexes them. This program is the other side of that: it is the thing a program with no allocator
would have to write before it could have one, which is why it is written with no allocator itself.

It is generic in what it holds, and that is the point rather than a flourish. A slab over one
hardcoded struct needs no `sizeof` at all — a literal block size would do — so the generic form is
what makes the measurement load-bearing, and it is the shape a real allocator has.

The free list is **intrusive**: a block that is free holds the address of the next free block in its
own first bytes, so the list costs no storage beside the region. That is what a slab allocator is,
and it is only writable because a `*u8` can be read as a `**u8` and back.

Deliberately not modelled: more than one block size, coalescing, and returning the region. The first
is a different allocator, and the other two need a heap this program is written without.


What writing it found
---------------------

**The language cannot demand that storage be aligned.** A `[N]u8` is aligned to one, because that is
what its element needs, and there is no `alignas`. So a region declared the obvious way is not
aligned for anything carved out of it, and every load through the resulting pointer is unaligned —
which x86 tolerates and which faults on some of the targets this language is for. The allocator
therefore rounds its own base up, exactly as a real one does, paying up to `alignof(T) - 1` bytes of
the region for it. That is the right behaviour for an allocator and the wrong reason to have written
it: an allocator rounds up because its *caller's* region is wherever it is, not because the language
could not say what it wanted. `alignof` made the rounding writable; a declaration attribute would
have made it unnecessary here.

**`sizeof` is what makes a container generic, and `alignof` is what makes it correct.** With only the
first, a slab over any `T` still lays its blocks at whatever offset the region began at. The two
arrived together for this reason rather than by tidiness.

**A block has to be wide enough to hold the list that threads it.** `sizeof(T) >= sizeof(*u8)` is a
real precondition — a slab of `u16` cannot store an address inside a free block — and it is a
`require` rather than a comment because both sides of it are constants the compiler already knows. A
language without `sizeof` could not have stated it at all.

**Nothing checks a reinterpreted pointer, and nothing should.** `ptr_cast` hands back a `*T` aimed at
bytes that hold no `T` yet; the caller writes one before reading one, and no rule here says so. That
is the raw tier behaving as `03` says it does — the same assertion `p[i]` past the end already is —
and it is why the whole file is greppable for the three operations that take on the risk.

**This module is allocator-free and still cannot say so — but the reason has changed.** When this was
written, `no alloc` was designed (`capabilities.md`, `13 §4`) and not built, so writing the clause
produced a diagnostic saying exactly that. **It is built now**, and `guide/bytecode`'s VM half carries
it, which was the other program that had recorded the same absence.

What keeps it out of *this* directory is the clause's own shape rather than its absence: a capability
is a property of the **module**, a module is a directory, and the clause has to appear in every file
of one. `main.sysl` next door renders a `str` on nearly every line of its checks, which allocates —
so the clause would be refused there and a module cannot carry it in half its files. `guide/kernel`
sits in exactly the same position and says so.

So the claim still lives in this paragraph rather than in the source, and that is still the weakest
place it could live — nothing checks it, and a `&T` added tomorrow would be accepted. The way to fix
it is no longer a language feature; it is moving the allocator into a module of its own, which is what
`guide/bytecode` did with its VM.

**An allocator may now call its function `alloc`, and this one does — the finding that said it could
not is discharged.** The word used to be reserved, because the capability clause was written as the
two ordinary words `no alloc`, so the most natural name in the whole program was the one name it
could not have and this file called it `take`. **Capabilities are attributes now** — `@no_alloc` and
`@requires(...)` (`13 §4`) — and an attribute's name is an ordinary identifier, so `alloc`, `no`,
`requires` and `link` all came back to every program at once. This file is the one that wanted the
word most, which is why it is the one that reports the change.

**The one thing the compiler still refuses is a `&T`.** An early draft tried to hand back a counted
reference, on the grounds that a slab block outlives its user. It cannot: ARC would have no count to
own, and the safe subset relies on a `&T` being a live object. So an allocator's result is a `*T` and
stays in the raw tier, which is where a caller reaching for one already is.


Reading a free block as a link
------------------------------

A block that is free holds the next free block's address in its own storage, so the list is reached
by reading the block as a pointer to a pointer. Both halves of that are here so the reinterpretation
appears twice in the file rather than at every use.

    link(b: *u8) -> **u8
        ptr_cast(b)
    end link

    next_free(b: *u8) -> *u8
        *link(b)
    end next_free


The slab
--------

A region carved into blocks of one type, and the head of the list of the ones nobody holds.

`first` is the aligned start rather than the region's own, so the region's first bytes may not belong
to any block — see `slab_over` below. `made` is how many blocks fitted after that.

    struct Slab[T]
        first: *T
        free: *u8
        made: usize


Carving the region
------------------

`slab_over` cuts `bytes` of the region at `region` into as many blocks of `T` as fit, and threads
every one of them onto the free list.

    slab_over[T](region: *u8, bytes: usize) -> Slab[T]

The precondition is the one the intrusive list imposes, and both sides of it are constants — so this
is a compile-time-known fact stated where the compiler can hold the caller to it.

        require sizeof(T) >= sizeof(*u8), "a free block has to be wide enough to hold the link through it"

Rounding the base up is what costs the region its first `lost` bytes, and `made` counts what fits
after that rather than what would have fit from the region's own start.

        var start = usize(region)
        var base = aligned_up(start, alignof(T))
        var lost = base - start
        var block = sizeof(T)
        var made = if bytes > lost then (bytes - lost) / block else 0usize
        var first: *u8 = ptr_cast(base)
        var head: *u8 = null
        var i = made

The blocks are linked **back to front**, so the head ends up at the lowest address and the first
allocation is the first block. Nothing requires that; it is done so the checks can say where a block
came from.

        while i > 0 do
            i -= 1

            var b: *u8 = &first[i * block]

            *link(b) = head
            head = b

        Slab(ptr_cast(first), head, made)
    end slab_over

The next address at or above `n` that `a` divides. `a` is a power of two here — every alignment is —
but the arithmetic is written the way that does not depend on it, since nothing in the type says so.

    aligned_up(n: usize, a: usize) -> usize
        (n + a - 1) / a * a
    end aligned_up


Taking and returning a block
----------------------------

`alloc` takes a block off the free list, or answers `null` where the region has none left. The result
is a `*T` aimed at storage holding no `T` yet, which is the whole of what an allocator does and the
whole of what makes it the raw tier.

    alloc[T](s: *Slab[T]) -> *T
        if s.free == null then return null

        var b = s.free

        s.free = next_free(b)
        ptr_cast(b)
    end alloc

`release` puts a block back, by writing the current head into the block itself.

    release[T](s: *Slab[T], p: *T)
        var b: *u8 = ptr_cast(p)

        *link(b) = s.free
        s.free = b
    end release


Asking about the region
-----------------------

How many blocks are on the free list, counted by walking it. The count is not kept as a field on
purpose: walking it is what asserts the list really is threaded through the blocks.

    free_count[T](s: *Slab[T]) -> usize
        var n = 0usize
        var w = s.free

        while w != null do
            n += 1
            w = next_free(w)

        n
    end free_count

Which block a pointer is, counted from the aligned start. Undefined for a pointer the slab did not
hand out, which is the raw tier's ordinary bargain.

    index_of[T](s: *Slab[T], p: *T) -> usize
        (usize(p) - usize(s.first)) / sizeof(T)
    end index_of
